#question(title: [Definition of limit of a sequence (continued)], shrink: true)[
  #set text(size: 12pt)

  Let $display({a_n}_(n = 0)^oo)$ be a
  sequence. Let $display(L in RR)$.  \
  Which statements are equivalent to
  $display(“ {a_n}_(n = 0)^oo --> L ”)$?

  10. $display(forall epsilon > 0)$, the
      interval
      $display((L - epsilon comma L + epsilon))$
      contains all the elements of the
      sequence, except the first few.
  + $display(forall epsilon > 0)$, the
    interval
    $display((L - epsilon comma L + epsilon))$
    contains infinitely many of the elements
    of the sequence.
  + $display(forall epsilon > 0)$, the
    interval
    $display((L - epsilon comma L + epsilon))$
    contains #emph[#text(fill: red)[almost
    all]] the elements of the sequence.
  + $display(forall epsilon > 0)$, the
    interval
    $display([L - epsilon comma L + epsilon])$
    contains #emph[#text(fill: red)[almost
    all]] the elements of the sequence.
  + Every interval that contains $L$ must
    contain #emph[#text(fill: red)[almost
    all]] all the elements of the sequence.
  + Every open interval that contains $L$
    must contain
    #emph[#text(fill: red)[almost all]] all
    the elements of the sequence.

  #emph[Notation:]
  “#emph[#text(fill: red)[almost all]]" =
  “all, except finitely many"
]
